## THE FACTS AT THE BASE (MEASURED unless marked; re-print at your base)
1. **The false comment.** `web/src/server/reviewDoor.ts:4` reads: `// proxy.ts (the page) and bff.ts (the calls) apply it in the "uat" mode only; Entra and the laptop's mock are not changed here.` (the file is 33 lines; lines 1-3 are the header). Why it is false: `web/src/components/shell/AppShell.tsx:81` reads `const openPage = (auth === "uat" || auth === "mock") && isReviewPage(pathname);` with its comment at `:79-80` ("On the review copies and the laptop, the held-feedback review page opens to any visitor (server/reviewDoor.ts): with no user it is shown on its own"), and `AppShell.tsx:7` imports `isReviewPage` from this file. True facts for the comment: `proxy.ts:86` (`if (auth.kind === "uat" && isReviewPage(path)) return false;`) and `bff.ts:437` (`const review = isReviewCall(...)`, inside the `auth.kind === "uat"` branch that opens at `bff.ts:433`) apply the door in the "uat" mode only; the shell also shows the page with no user in the "mock" mode (the laptop); Entra (the production sign-in) is not changed. In the mock mode `proxy.ts:82` already returns false for every page (no gate) and the BFF mints no assertion: those are not changed either (DERIVED).
2. **The guard's place.** `bff.ts:404-405` is `const blocked = guard(req); if (blocked) return blocked;`: the same-origin guard runs BEFORE the mode, the Entra bearer (`:417`), the UAT sign-in branch (`:433-445`, whose no-level-and-not-a-review-call answer is the 401 at `:438-442`) and the forward. `guard.ts:49-54` is the guard: any of POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE (`guard.ts:5`) with a `Sec-Fetch-Site` that is not `same-origin`, or an `Origin` that is not the request's host, is 403. **The hole B1309-2 names:** if the guard were moved to after the sign-in branch (after `bff.ts:445`), a cross-site write with no session to a CLOSED path would answer 401 (from `:438`) instead of 403, and the existing row `reviewDoor.test.ts:123` would NOT notice, because it sends only the OPEN paths (`OPEN.filter(([m]) => m !== "GET")`, `:125`), for which `review` is true and the guard still runs before the forward. (DERIVED by reading; the plants below measure it.)
3. **HEAD.** `reviewDoor.ts:29-31` is `if (method === "GET") ...; if (method === "POST") ...; if (method === "DELETE") ...;` and `:32` `return false`, so HEAD is not a review call. `reviewDoor.test.ts` has no HEAD row (`/usr/bin/grep -c -w HEAD` over the raw file printed 0 at 10:2x; control: `-w method` over the same file printed 20, so the reader fires; you re-print both). HEAD is not in `MUTATING` (`guard.ts:5`), so the guard does not look at it; for a visitor with a level the BFF forwards it with that level (`bff.ts:443`); with none it is 401 (`:438`). DERIVED: expected `HEAD` held list and `HEAD` closed item answers are "401 0 null" with no level and "200 1 <own level>" with one (the stub upstream answers 200); the first run of your rows at the base shows whether that holds.
4. **The test file.** `web/src/server/reviewDoor.test.ts` is 173 lines with 8 `it(` rows (`:99, :109, :119, :123, :136, :152, :161, :166`) in two `describe`s (`:98`, `:148`). Its helpers: `ID` `:24`, `VISITORS` `:27` (no level, then the four), `call()` `:29`, `send()` `:35` (returns `{status, calls, level, auth}`; `level` is the minted assertion's level, `null` when no upstream call), `OPEN` `:46`, `OPEN_VARIANTS` `:54`, `CLOSED` `:63-94` (30 entries: 17 GET and **13 not-GET**, counted by `awk` over `:64-93` with `grep -c -v '"GET"'` and `grep -c '"GET"'`; the 13 are one PUT, one PATCH, seven DELETEs and four POSTs, `:77-87` and `:89-90`). The existing cross-site row is `:123-134` (its header variants, `:126`).
